WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.399
You can't have a good Pixar movie without
a bunch of Easter eggs, right?

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:11.540
There are more than a few to spot in Finding
Dory, the long-awaited 2016 sequel to Finding

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.540
Nemo.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.219
Let's dive in and find out what you might
have missed during the movie.

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:19.160
And it should go without saying, but there
are ​spoilers ahead​.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.990
Darla strikes back?

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.810
When Dory gets taken into the Marine Life
Center's quarantine room, she looks over at

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:30.039
a bulletin board with a photo of a very familiar
girl with headgear pinned to it.

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:34.010
That's right, if you're quick enough, you
can spot a picture of Darla, the fish-tormenting

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.710
girl from the original Finding Nemo.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.260
Of course, just why there's a picture of an
Australian girl in a Californian Marine Life

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.190
Center, we just can't answer.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:46.760
Maybe her reputation for torturing our finny
friends has spread across the Pacific Ocean.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:47.760
Wired

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.110
The movie's two sea lion characters, Fluke
and Rudder, are voiced by Idris Elba and Dominic

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.470
West, respectively.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.530
These two actors starred in the HBO series
The Wire, with Idris playing Russell 'Stringer'

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.670
Bell and West playing Detective Jimmy McNulty.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:04.500
As Fluke and Rudder, they sit on a rock and
help Nemo and Marlin find their way into the

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.219
Marine Life Center, all while keeping their
spot safe from other sea lions like Gerald:

00:01:09.219 --> 00:01:11.439
"Ha, mate, don't you worry-"

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:15.350
(Sea lions yelling)

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.939
Maybe not the same level of drama as ​The
Wire​, but you take what you can get in

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:21.000
a kid's movie.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.000
A113

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.280
Fluke and Rudder also happen to hide another
Easter egg.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.790
As pointed out by the Pixar Post blog, merchandise
featuring the characters have tags on their

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:31.840
flippers that read "A1" and "13."

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.259
That's a reference to the classroom at the
California Institute of the Arts where many

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:39.479
of the Pixar and Disney animators learned
their craft, and it's appeared in every Pixar

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:43.369
movie ever released, not to mention more than
a few Disney movies, too.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.889
The Pizza Planet Truck rides again

00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:49.720
You can also spot the the famed Pizza Planet
Truck from the original ​Toy Story​ when

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.119
Hank and Dory steal the transport truck headed
for Cleveland.

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:57.149
We're not sure how many different Pizza Planet
franchises there are throughout time and space,

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:59.420
but this beat-up, yellow truck sure gets around.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.149
It's appeared in just about every Pixar flick.

00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:03.799
Wall-E calendar

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:08.170
In the scene where Hank the septopus hangs
from the pipes above the Open Ocean tank,

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.959
there's a small desk in the top right corner
of the screen.

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:13.110
Above that desk is a calendar that reads "Wall-E."

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.849
It would have also been cool to see some Buy'n'Large
items littered around, giving some credence

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:22.030
to the theory that these Pixar movies are
all part of the same shared (and ultimately

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.030
doomed) world.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.310
But, sure, a calendar is fine too.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:26.310
Herbie the Love Bug

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.450
Be on the lookout for a familiar face...kind
of.

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:34.350
Buried down on the ocean floor is the world's
most famous 1963 Volkswagen Beetle, Herbie

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.510
the Love Bug.

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.319
Of course, as cute as the deep Disney reference
is...it's actually a pretty creepy spot for

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:40.780
Herbie's final resting place.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.470
Who knows, maybe this is one of those Easter
eggs that Pixar sticks into their movies that

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.069
hints at a future film.

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:48.140
Maybe they'll take on a Herbie movie of their
own?

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.430
Or better yet, maybe he'll make his comeback
in Cars 3...

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.840
Ride reminder

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.950
According to Ultimate Orlando, one of the
pipes in the Marine Life Center reads "Seawater

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.049
Supply TL59."

00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:03.599
That's a reference to the Finding Nemo Submarine
Voyage at Disneyland in Anaheim, California.

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:07.390
This explanation doesn't stop there, either:
the attraction used to be called simply the

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.870
Submarine Voyage, but was re-themed after
the success of the film.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:15.690
The pipe was included on the line to the ride
as a tribute to its former, non-Nemo-themed

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:16.690
existence.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.379
Return of the Tank Gang

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:21.930
If you managed to sit through the credits,
enjoying various snippets of Hank using his

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.219
camouflaging abilities in different locations,
then you'd be treated to a post-credits scene

00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:29.739
featuring the sea lions Fluke and Rudder hanging
out on their rock.

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:31.939
And who should happen to come swimming past
them?

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:33.769
The Tank Gang from Finding Nemo.

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:38.069
Brad Garrett, Willem Dafoe and the rest came
back to reprise their roles so we could see

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:39.650
where the gang wound up.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.620
Hank, the greatest Easter egg of them all

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.189
Did you know that Hank the septopus actually
appeared in every one of Pixar's movies without

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:49.060
anyone ever realizing it?

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.060
It's true!

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.430
Well, at least according to Finding Dory​
director Andrew Stanton and Hank's voice actor,

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.569
Ed O'Neill, who appeared in a video explaining
that Hank had been camouflaged into every

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:02.609
Pixar movie since the first ​Toy Story​
hit theaters way back in 1995.

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:04.310
Wait, what?

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.760
Oh, that video was published on April 1, 2016.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.500
So...ya never mind.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.579
Thanks for watching!

00:04:10.579 --> 00:04:13.780
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.169
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Easter eggs we missed…

